  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIncreasing moisture will make a return to the region as southerly \nreturn flow increases behind a passing sfc high and with a Bermuda \nHigh off the SE coast. Temperatures will also warm some from values \nearlier in the week. \n\nAs heat and humidity build across the region, weak passing \ndisturbances/shortwave troughs will allow for the development of \nshowers and thunderstorms starting on Thursday. Heavy downpours will \nbe possible at times as PWat values increase to 1.75\"+ starting \ntonight. The first of the passing shortwave troughs will cross the \narea on Thursday, increasing the potential for showers and \nthunderstorms to develop during the afternoon hours. Most of (if not \nall of) the area is under a 'Marginal Risk' on Thursday from both \nSPC and WPC for severe thunderstorms and excessive runoff, \nrespectively. The potential for strong to severe thunderstorms is \nexpected to be limited by less than favorable shear values of sub 30 \nkts. Instability on the order of 1,500-2,000 J/kg of CAPE, will help \nwith some shower/thunderstorm development as the shortwave troughs \npass over the area, but any strong to severe storms should be \nisolated. \n\nShowers with some thunderstorms will return for Friday as a slow \nmoving frontal boundary approaches from the west, weakening as it \ntracks into the region. With the moisture laden atmosphere still in \nplace over the region and the slow moving front, the potential for \nheavier downpours will continue for the day on Friday. Some of the \nnewer guidance stalls the front over the area through the day on \nSaturday, causing showers and the potential for scattered showers to \nlinger through the first half of the weekend. An incoming larger \ntrough will eventually push what is left of the front out of the \narea late Saturday. A 'Marginal Risk' for excessive rainfall will \ncontinue for Friday and Saturday with the moisture still in place \nover the region. \n\nIncreasing humidity levels along with warm temperatures will start \nto increase heat index values through the end of the week. The \nprimary areas of concern for areas approaching advisory levels will \nbe the far southern PA counties. But even for these areas, only a \nsmall portion of these areas will be close to advisory levels.\nOtherwise areas of upper 80s to low 90s will be possible at\ntimes each afternoon through the end of the week. High \ntemperatures through the first half of the weekend will reach \nthe low to mid 80s for most areas, with some of the lower \nelevation areas approaching the upper 80s at times.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe flow will become mainly zonal starting Sunday with shortwave \ntroughs and sfc lows passing across or just north of the region. \nThis will result in showers and scattered thunderstorms to return \nlater in the day on Sunday, continuing into the work week. Warm and \nhumid conditions will remain mostly in place through the start of \nnext week.",
